# 05:00 Riyadh, DAILY — the hour is inherited and argued; the period was
# weekly until 2026-08-08 and is derived now.
#
# WHY 05:00, unchanged. It is after the 03:30 daily backup, so §8's freshness
# check reads a snapshot taken ninety minutes earlier: a backup run that failed
# last night is caught this morning. And Sunday's firing still lands an hour
# before the operator's weekly report (06:00–12:00) — the first working day of
# the Saudi week — so a red result is already on his phone when he sits down to
# read the week.
#
# WHY DAILY, and why weekly was wrong. The old note here said, in as many
# words, that «nothing here changes hour to hour except the backup age, the
# threshold on that is two days» — and then sampled it every seven. A
# two-day threshold read on a seven-day period is not a threshold, it is a
# decoration: a chain that stops the hour after Sunday's probe is «fine» for
# five days in which nothing at all is looking, and the answer «the newest
# backup is one day old» is one the operator believes because we told him.
#
#   * The probe's period IS the detection latency for this failure. A backup
#     that FAILS pages him on its own — career-backup.service carries
#     OnFailure= — so what §8 exists for is the silent shape: a timer that
#     stopped firing, a repository that quietly stopped receiving snapshots, a
#     unit disabled by a half-finished deploy. None of those produce a failed
#     unit, so none of them produce an alert of any kind, and nothing else on
#     this host reads a backup's age unattended (the watchtower's health screen
#     reads it only when the operator taps, which releases fastest for the
#     operator who is already watching).
#   * The arithmetic. Weekly: the chain stops after a probe and is reported
#     seven days later. Daily: age 1d and 2d still pass, 3d fails — so the
#     worst case falls from seven days to three, and «two days» becomes a
#     number that means something.
#
# WHAT THE EXTRA FREQUENCY COSTS. Seven runs a week instead of one, of a
# script that is read-only by contract. Eight of the nine checks are local and
# take milliseconds (fontconfig, the RLS catalog, the migration head, five
# `systemctl show`s). The ninth lists snapshots over the network — one restic
# metadata call, six more B2 class-B transactions a week, which is not a
# rounding error, it is below one. Nothing here restores anything: the
# expensive proof-by-restore is career-restore-test.timer and it stays monthly.
#
# AND WHAT STOPS IT BECOMING NOISE, because a probe that fires more often is
# an ALERT that fires more often, and the other side of «an alert nobody
# reads» is an alert too frequent to be read. Three things:
#
#   * every one of the nine checks is a structural fault that does NOT
#     self-heal — backups stopped, RLS dropped, fonts gone, the database off
#     the migration head, a unit not enabled. There is no blip in this set. A
#     daily reminder of an unrepaired fault is escalation, not repetition;
#   * the volume it can reach is one message a day. This repository already
#     treats one an HOUR as the acceptable ceiling for a permanently wedged
#     worker (REPEAT_S=3600 in scripts/alert_unit_failure.sh, 24 a day against
#     an untreated 283), so this is a twenty-fourth of a rate that was already
#     argued down to «reminders, not repetitions»;
#   * the failing verdict carries WHICH of the nine checks failed, so two
#     consecutive days are not two identical messages.
#
#   The residual, stated rather than hidden: alert_unit_failure.sh keys
#   «still broken» versus «broke again» on INCIDENT_GAP_S=900, a number
#   derived from the always-on units' 305s kill cadence. Every scheduled
#   oneshot on this host — this probe, the backup, the nightly run — fails
#   further apart than that, so a second consecutive red is announced as
#   «تعطلت من جديد بعد فترة سلامة» when nothing recovered. That is a
#   pre-existing defect of the notifier and not of this schedule (it already
#   misreports career-backup and career-engine-nightly, both daily); this
#   timer adds one more unit to the class rather than creating it.
[Unit]
Description=Daily restore/health verification at 05:00 Riyadh (9 checks)

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 05:00:00 Asia/Riyadh
# A server that was down this morning is precisely the one worth checking, so
# a missed run is caught up rather than skipped.
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
